801 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099801 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "81d7abef-1e5f-5e7e-a713-da16f17f6fd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099802 #edb/ref 1080 "No such $k$ exists" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099802 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099802 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f2d497f-046e-5ff8-9cc4-36ed95644117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099803 #edb/ref 1080 "± 3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099803 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099803 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "932f68d0-3981-5043-bca8-0d34e2a7c58c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099804 #edb/ref 1080 "± 2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099804 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099804 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b489cad0-a823-5608-8178-c647f5a4a9d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099805 #edb/ref 1080 "± 5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "139fc8af-4064-5873-aff1-d72d9a72cf0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099806 #edb/ref 1080 "± 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099806 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099806 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "200d7f0a-daed-5888-8a11-0597758e9c97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100618 #edb/ref 1069 "e-267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100618 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value(s) of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-1$?\n${\\frac{x^{3} + 1}{kx + k},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne - 1 \\\\ \\frac{k}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x =-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100618 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100618 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{3} + 1}{kx + k} &= ((x + 1)(x^{2} - x + 1))/(k(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 1)(x^{2} - x + 1))/(k(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2} - x + 1}{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can compute the limit at $x=-1$, and get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} g(x) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} \\left(\\dfrac{x^2-x+1}{k}\\right) = \\dfrac{3}{k}\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 1)g(x) &= g(-1) \\\\\n\\frac{3}{k} &= \\frac{k}{3} \\\\\n9 &= k^{2} \\\\\n± 3 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-1$, the value of $k$ must be either $-3$ or $3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100619 #edb/ref 1069 "e-268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100619 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the values of $k$ for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$.\n${\\frac{x^{2} + 4x - 12}{x^{2} - 4},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\\\ 2^{kx},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100619 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100619 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})\\frac{x^{2} + 4x - 12}{x^{2} - 4} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2^{-})((x - 2)(x + 6))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2^{-})((x - 2)(x + 6))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2^{-})\\frac{x + 6}{x + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 + 6}{2 + 2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})2^{kx} \\\\\n&= 2^{2k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n\n$$\nf(2) = 2^{2k}\n$$\n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) \\\\\n2^{2k} &= 2 \\\\\n2^{2k} &= 2^{1} \\\\\n2k &= 1 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nremoves the discontinuity at $x=2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100803 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100804 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101077 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101077 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101077 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101077 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101077 #edb/ref 1045 "Removing Discontinuities From Rational Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101077 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101077 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101077 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4b7be0a1-49cf-57e8-8286-c52f0151df07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101440 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x =-2$?\n$g(x) = {\\frac{x^{2} + 5x + 6}{kx + 2k},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne - 2 \\\\ k,\\end{vmatrix}x =-2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab43e107-00fc-5630-8abd-2dc86272117f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101570 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101570 #edb/ref 1061 1031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d10fc2dc-3be0-5724-8892-7273ab4b7322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101571 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101571 #edb/ref 1061 1032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101571 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "080a88b1-8b9c-503a-a435-862f18fa647f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101571 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101572 #edb/ref 1061 1035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101572 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7867d9d8-2d45-52ba-b25f-cdcdfeac2305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101572 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ k,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1$. So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for\n\n$$\nx \\neq 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 1}{1 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1$, the function is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nf(1)=k\n$$\n\n, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1$, we need to choose\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1065 548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81a7db1c-98c9-5308-96d8-457b407671db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
